Task context: We are porting this C++ program to pure Rust (2021 edition, rustc 1.75.0, ZERO external crates, std only):

```cpp
#include <immer/vector.hpp>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
int main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
    int n = (argc > 1) ? std::stoi(argv[1]) : 11;
    auto v = immer::vector<int>{};
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) { v = v.push_back(i + 1); }
    auto sum = 0;
    for (auto x : v) { sum += x; }
    std::cout << sum << std::endl;
    auto v2 = immer::vector<int>{};
    for (int i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i) { v2 = v2.push_back(v[i] * 2); }
    sum = 0;
    for (auto x : v2) { sum += x; }
    std::cout << sum << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

The reference compiled C++ binary is at /workspace/dataset/test11_executable — you MAY run it with any arguments to observe behavior.
HARD RULE: do NOT read any immer library source code (it is not present anyway). This is a black-box re-implementation: infer immer::vector's contract from the interface above and re-implement from scratch with std only.

immer::vector is a persistent (immutable) vector: a bit-partitioned vector trie (RRB-tree / Clojure-style PersistentVector with a tail buffer), branching factor 32 (5 bits). push_back returns a NEW vector sharing structure with the old one; the old one remains valid and unchanged. Operations: size() -> size_t, operator[](size_t) -> const T&, begin()/end() forward iteration, default construction.


You are Judge 2, evaluating through the lens of: **correctness and boundary-case risk**.

Three candidate designs were produced:

--- DESIGN A (Clojure/immer-style persistent bit-partitioned vector trie (branching factor 32) with a shared tail leaf, Rc-based path copying, radix indexing, and a leaf-caching forward iterator — validated against the reference binary via a working prototype) ---
{
  "approach_name": "Clojure/immer-style persistent bit-partitioned vector trie (branching factor 32) with a shared tail leaf, Rc-based path copying, radix indexing, and a leaf-caching forward iterator — validated against the reference binary via a working prototype",
  "data_structure": "CONSTANTS: `const BITS: u32 = 5; const B: usize = 32; const MASK: usize = 31;`

NODE — a two-variant enum, one heap allocation per node, shared via `Rc`:
```rust
enum Node<T> {
    Inner(Vec<Rc<Node<T>>>),  // 1..=32 children, all at the same level
    Leaf(Vec<T>),             // 1..=32 elements (0 only for the empty vector's tail)
}
```
Accessors `fn inner(&self) -> &Vec<Rc<Node<T>>>` and `fn leaf(&self) -> &Vec<T>` panic on the wrong variant; the level/shift bookkeeping guarantees they are never mis-called (the prototype asserted this across 105 cases and never tripped).
Rationale for `Vec` over `[Option<T>; 32]`: no `T: Default`/`Copy` bound, no `Option` tag per slot, and `len()` doubles as the "how many children are populated" counter, which the push path needs. Optional later optimization: `Box<[T]>` / `Box<[Rc<Node<T>>]>` to drop the unused capacity word (see pitfalls).

VECTOR — 4 words + 2 Rc pointers:
```rust
pub struct Vector<T> {
    root:  Rc<Node<T>>,  // ALWAYS Node::Inner. Holds elements [0, tail_offset)
    tail:  Rc<Node<T>>,  // ALWAYS Node::Leaf. Holds elements [tail_offset, size)
    size:  usize,        // total element count
    shift: u32,          // bit-shift for the ROOT level; 5, 10, 15, ...
}
```
`Clone` is derived-equivalent: bump both Rc refcounts, copy `size`/`shift`. O(1).

LEVEL/SHIFT CONVENTION (pin this down; it is the #1 source of off-by-one bugs):
`shift` is the shift applied at the root. A node at level `L` selects its child with `(i >> L) & MASK`; its children are at level `L - 5`; leaves are at level 0. So the root is at level `shift`, and tree depth (in Inner levels) is `shift / 5`.

| shift | root contains        | tree-part capacity      | depth grows at size |
|-------|----------------------|-------------------------|---------------------|
| 5     | Inner of Leaves      | 32^2 = 1 024            | 1 056               |
| 10    | Inner of Inner/Leaf  | 32^3 = 32 768           | 32 800              |
| 15    | ...                  | 32^4 = 1 048 576        | 1 048 608           |
| 20    | ...                  | 32^5 = 33 554 432       | 33 554 464          |

Tree-part capacity at a given shift is exactly `1 << (shift + BITS)`.

KEY DERIVED QUANTITY: `fn tail_offset(&self) -> usize { self.size - self.tail.leaf().len() }` — the number of elements living in the trie. Because only *full* 32-element leaves are ever pushed into the trie, `tail_offset` is always a multiple of 32.

EMPTY VECTOR: `root = Rc::new(Node::Inner(vec![]))`, `tail = Rc::new(Node::Leaf(vec![]))`, `size = 0`, `shift = 5`. This is the only state where a Leaf may be empty; the empty root is never descended into because `tail_offset == 0` routes every index to the tail.

PUBLIC API (mirrors the C++ surface exactly): `new()`, `size() -> usize`, `get(usize) -> &T`, `impl Index<usize>`, `push_back(&self, T) -> Vector<T>` (takes `&self`, returns a new vector — the receiver is untouched), `iter()`, `impl IntoIterator for &Vector<T>`, `impl Default`, `impl Clone`.",
  "key_invariants": [
    "`root` is always `Node::Inner`; `tail` is always `Node::Leaf`. Never violated, so the variant accessors never panic.",
    "`shift >= BITS` (minimum 5) and `shift % BITS == 0`. `shift` is the level of the root; children of a node at level L are at level L-5; leaves are at level 0.",
    "`tail_offset == size - tail.len()` and `tail_offset % 32 == 0` — only complete 32-element leaves are ever moved into the trie.",
    "`1 <= tail.len() <= 32` whenever `size > 0`. `tail.len() == 0` iff `size == 0`. The tail is never empty for a non-empty vector, so `push_back` never has to synthesize a tail from the tree.",
    "`tail_offset <= 1 << (shift + BITS)` — the trie at the current shift can hold the whole tree part. Equality means the root is exactly full and the next leaf push must grow depth.",
    "The trie is a PERFECT tree except along its right edge: every Leaf reachable from the root holds exactly 32 elements, and every Inner node except those on the rightmost path holds exactly 32 children. This is what makes radix indexing valid with no per-node size table (unlike a relaxed RRB-tree).",
    "The leaf slot targeted by a tail flush is always fresh: at level 5, `(tail_offset >> 5) & MASK == children.len()`, so the insert is always a `push` onto the end, never an overwrite. (Asserted in the prototype; never tripped.)",
    "Nodes are immutable once wrapped in `Rc`. Every mutation allocates new nodes along one root-to-leaf path (path copying) and shares all off-path subtrees by refcount bump. No `Rc::get_mut`/`make_mut` anywhere.",
    "Element `i` lives in the tail iff `i >= tail_offset`, otherwise in the trie. `get` must test this FIRST.",
    "Structural sharing is real and load-bearing: verified empirically that 200,000 coexisting versions of a 200k-element vector consume 49 MB rather than the 78 GB deep copying would require."
  ],
  "push_back_algorithm": "`pub fn push_back(&self, x: T) -> Vector<T> where T: Clone`. Three cases, exactly as in Clojure's `PersistentVector.cons` / immer's `push_back`.

```rust
let tl = self.tail.leaf().len();
if tl < B {
    // ---- CASE A: tail has room (the common case, 31 of every 32 pushes) ----
    let mut t = self.tail.leaf().clone();   // copy <=32 elements
    t.push(x);
    Vector { root: self.root.clone(),       // whole trie shared by refcount bump
             tail: Rc::new(Node::Leaf(t)),
             size: self.size + 1,
             shift: self.shift }
} else {
    // tail is full: it becomes a leaf of the trie, and x starts a fresh tail
    let toff = self.size - B;               // == tail_offset before the push
    let leaf = self.tail.clone();           // ZERO element copying: Rc bump only
    let (new_root, new_shift) =
        if (toff >> BITS) + 1 > (1usize << self.shift) {
            // ---- CASE C: root is full -> grow depth by one level ----
            (Rc::new(Node::Inner(vec![
                 self.root.clone(),                  // old root becomes child 0
                 new_path(self.shift, leaf),         // spine down to the new leaf
             ])),
             self.shift + BITS)
        } else {
            // ---- CASE B: root has room -> path-copy the right edge ----
            (push_leaf(&self.root, self.shift, toff, leaf), self.shift)
        };
    Vector { root: new_root, tail: Rc::new(Node::Leaf(vec![x])),
             size: self.size + 1, shift: new_shift }
}
```

CASE A — tail not full. Clone the tail's `Vec<T>` (at most 32 elements, hence the `T: Clone` bound), append, rewrap. The entire trie is shared by a single refcount increment. O(32) copy, one allocation.

CASE A note on the empty vector: `size == 0` gives `tl == 0 < 32`, so the first push takes Case A and produces `tail = [x]`. No special-casing needed.

CASE B — tail full, root has room. The full tail leaf is adopted into the trie unchanged (`self.tail.clone()` — a pure refcount bump; nothing is copied, which is why the flush is O(log n) and not O(32)). `push_leaf` copies one root-to-leaf path:
```rust
fn push_leaf<T>(node: &Rc<Node<T>>, level: u32, toff: usize,
                leaf: Rc<Node<T>>) -> Rc<Node<T>> {
    let idx = (toff >> level) & MASK;
    let mut ch = node.inner().clone();   // shallow: clones Rc handles, not subtrees
    if idx < ch.len() {
        ch[idx] = push_leaf(&ch[idx], level - BITS, toff, leaf);  // descend
    } else {
        debug_assert_eq!(idx, ch.len());
        ch.push(new_path(level - BITS, leaf));   // fresh spine from here down
    }
    Rc::new(Node::Inner(ch))
}
```
`ch.len()` is the populated-child count, so `idx < ch.len()` means "that subtree already exists, recurse into it" and `idx == ch.len()` means "start a new spine here". Use `toff` (not `size`, and not Clojure's `cnt - 1`) for the radix digits: because `toff` is a multiple of 32, `(toff >> level) & MASK == ((toff + 31) >> level) & MASK` for every `level >= 5`, so this is equivalent to Clojure's `cnt-1` formulation but obviously correct. At `level == 5` the invariant `idx == ch.len()` always holds and `new_path(0, leaf)` returns `leaf` itself, so the bottom level needs no special case.

```rust
fn new_path<T>(level: u32, leaf: Rc<Node<T>>) -> Rc<Node<T>> {
    if level == 0 { leaf }
    else { Rc::new(Node::Inner(vec![new_path(level - BITS, leaf)])) }
}
```
`new_path(level, leaf)` builds a subtree whose ROOT SITS AT `level`, containing just `leaf`. Called as `new_path(level - BITS, ...)` from a parent at `level`.

CASE C — root full, depth increases. Overflow test: `(toff >> BITS) + 1 > (1usize << self.shift)`, i.e. "would the leaf count after this push exceed the 32^(shift/5) leaves the current root can address?". Equivalent formulations: `toff == 1 << (shift + BITS)`. The new root is an Inner node at level `shift + 5` with exactly two children: the old root (child 0, shared wholesale by refcount — the entire previous trie is reused) and `new_path(shift, leaf)`, a fresh one-child-per-level spine down to the new leaf. Child index 1 is provably correct: overflow occurs exactly when `toff == 1 << (shift+5)`, so `(toff >> (shift+5)) & MASK == 1`.

COST: amortized O(1) element copying (Case A copies ≤32 elements; the 1-in-32 flush copies zero elements), plus O(log32 n) ≤ 6 node allocations for the path copy. The old vector is fully valid and unchanged afterward. Empirically: 40M pushes in 3.5 s vs 1.4 s for the C++ reference — same order, and the gap is `Vec`-per-node allocation overhead, not algorithmic.",
  "index_algorithm": "Radix (bit-partitioned) descent, with the tail short-circuited first. Both `get` and the iterator go through one shared helper that returns the whole containing leaf, so the iterator can amortize the descent over 32 elements.

```rust
/// Returns (leaf slice containing element i, global index of that leaf's element 0).
fn leaf_for(&self, i: usize) -> (&[T], usize) {
    let toff = self.tail_offset();
    if i >= toff {
        return (self.tail.leaf(), toff);     // in the tail: O(1), no descent
    }
    let mut n = &self.root;
    let mut level = self.shift;
    while level > 0 {
        n = &n.inner()[(i >> level) & MASK];
        level -= BITS;
    }
    (n.leaf(), i & !MASK)                    // leaf base = i rounded down to 32
}

pub fn get(&self, i: usize) -> &T {
    assert!(i < self.size, "index {} out of bounds for length {}", i, self.size);
    let (leaf, base) = self.leaf_for(i);
    &leaf[i - base]
}
impl<T: Clone> std::ops::Index<usize> for Vector<T> {
    type Output = T;
    fn index(&self, i: usize) -> &T { self.get(i) }
}
```

Order of operations matters: the `i >= toff` test must come FIRST. For the empty vector (and for any vector with fewer than 33 elements) `toff == 0`, so every index routes to the tail and the empty root is never dereferenced.

The trie part is a perfect tree apart from its right edge, so index digits need no size table: the 5-bit group `(i >> level) & MASK` selects the child at each level, and the low 5 bits `i & MASK` select within the leaf. `i & !MASK` (equivalently `i - (i & MASK)`) is the leaf's base index — valid because every leaf inside the trie holds exactly 32 elements.

Cost: `shift/5` pointer hops, i.e. 2 hops up to 1 024 elements, 3 up to 32 768, 4 up to 1 048 576. Lifetimes flow naturally through `Rc` deref: `&'a self` yields `&'a [T]` with no `unsafe`, because holding `&self` keeps every `Rc` on the path alive.

Note on the C++ side: `for (int i = 0; i < v.size(); ++i)` compares `int` against `size_t`, promoting `i` to unsigned. Since `i` is never negative here this is exactly `for i in 0..v.size()` in Rust.",
  "iteration_algorithm": "A leaf-caching forward iterator: hold a borrowed slice of the current 32-element leaf and only re-descend when crossing a leaf boundary. This gives amortized O(1) per element (one descent per 32 elements) with no explicit path stack and no `unsafe`.

```rust
pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    v:    &'a Vector<T>,
    i:    usize,       // global index of the next element
    leaf: &'a [T],     // cached leaf; starts empty to force a refill
    base: usize,       // global index of leaf[0]
    end:  usize,       // base + leaf.len()
}

impl<'a, T: Clone> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        if self.i >= self.v.size { return None; }
        if self.i >= self.end {                          // crossed a leaf boundary
            let (l, b) = self.v.leaf_for(self.i);
            self.leaf = l; self.base = b; self.end = b + l.len();
        }
        let r = &self.leaf[self.i - self.base];
        self.i += 1;
        Some(r)
    }
    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {
        let rem = self.v.size - self.i; (rem, Some(rem))
    }
}
impl<'a, T: Clone> ExactSizeIterator for Iter<'a, T> {}

impl<T: Clone> Vector<T> {
    pub fn iter(&self) -> Iter<'_, T> {
        Iter { v: self, i: 0, leaf: &[], base: 0, end: 0 }
    }
}
impl<'a, T: Clone> IntoIterator for &'a Vector<T> {
    type Item = &'a T; type IntoIter = Iter<'a, T>;
    fn into_iter(self) -> Iter<'a, T> { self.iter() }
}
```

Initializing `leaf: &[]` with `end: 0` forces a refill on the first `next()` and makes the empty vector terminate immediately (`i == 0 >= size == 0` → `None`), so no special-casing.

The transition from trie leaves into the tail is automatic: once `i` reaches `tail_offset`, `leaf_for` returns the tail slice and `end` becomes `size`.

IMPORTANT — must implement `IntoIterator` for `&'a Vector<T>`, NOT for `Vector<T>` by value. The C++ program iterates `v` (`for (auto x : v)`) and then keeps using it (`v.size()`, `v[i]`) in the second loop, so iteration must not consume. In Rust this is `for x in v.iter()` or `for x in &v`, yielding `&T`; the C++ `auto x` copy becomes `*x`.

Do NOT implement iteration as `(0..size).map(|i| self.get(i))` — that re-descends from the root for every element, turning O(n) into O(n log n) and roughly 4x-ing the 20M-element runtime.",
  "module_layout": [
    "`Cargo.toml` — `[package] name/version`, `edition = "2021"`, `rust-version = "1.75"`, and an EMPTY `[dependencies]` section (zero external crates). Add `[profile.release] overflow-checks = false` only as belt-and-braces; correctness must not depend on it because all arithmetic uses explicit `wrapping_*`.",
    "`src/main.rs` — the program: `stoi`-compatible arg parsing (default `n = 11` when `argc <= 1`), the two push/sum loops with explicit wrapping arithmetic, and `println!` + flush per output line. Declares `mod vector; mod stoi;`.",
    "`src/vector/mod.rs` — `pub struct Vector<T>` and its inherent impls: `new`, `size`, `tail_offset`, `leaf_for`, `get`, `iter`; plus `impl Index<usize>`, `impl Clone`, `impl Default`, `impl IntoIterator for &Vector<T>`, and (optional, nice for tests) `impl Debug`/`FromIterator`. Declares `mod node; mod iter;` and re-exports `pub use iter::Iter;`.",
    "`src/vector/node.rs` — `pub(crate) enum Node<T>`, the `BITS`/`B`/`MASK` constants, the `inner()`/`leaf()` accessors, and the free functions `new_path` and `push_leaf`. All the bit-twiddling lives here and nowhere else.",
    "`src/stoi.rs` — `pub fn stoi(s: &str) -> Result<i32, StoiError>` replicating `std::stoi`, and a helper that mimics an uncaught C++ exception (exact stderr text + abort). Kept separate so it can be unit-tested against the reference binary's observed behavior.",
    "`src/vector/tests.rs` (or `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` inside `mod.rs`) — unit tests for: the depth-growth boundaries (sizes 1055/1056/1057, 32799/32800/32801, 1048607/1048608/1048609); `get(i) == i+1` for all i at each of those sizes; iterator order and length equal to indexed order; and the persistence property that N intermediate versions held simultaneously each retain exactly their own prefix.",
    "`tests/differential.rs` (optional integration test) — shells out to `/workspace/dataset/test11_executable` and compares stdout for a list of n values plus random n, exactly as I did while validating the prototype."
  ],
  "pitfalls": [
    "SIGNED OVERFLOW IS LOAD-BEARING AND WILL PANIC IN DEBUG BUILDS. The reference relies on `int` wraparound; I confirmed it: n=1048576 prints `524288` (true sum 549 756 338 176 ≡ 524 288 mod 2^32), n=65536 prints `-2147450880`, n=46341 prints `1073767311` then `-2147432674` (first sum positive, second wrapped negative), n=92681 prints `-37075` then `-74150`. Plain `sum += x` panics with 'attempt to add with overflow' under `cargo build`/`cargo test`. Use `wrapping_add`/`wrapping_mul` explicitly so debug and release agree; do NOT rely on release-mode wrapping or on an `overflow-checks = false` profile flag.",
    "`v[i] * 2` needs `wrapping_mul(2)` as well, not just the accumulator. It only overflows for elements above 2^30 (requiring n > 1.07e9, impractical to run) but silent-panic-vs-wrap divergence there is still a latent bug.",
    "SHIFT/LEVEL OFF-BY-ONE is the classic failure. Fix the convention once: `shift` is the ROOT's level; a node at level L indexes with `(i >> L) & MASK`; children are at `L - 5`; leaves are at level 0; `new_path(level, leaf)` returns a subtree whose root is AT `level`, so callers pass `level - BITS`. Getting this wrong yields a tree that works up to 1024 elements and breaks at 1056.",
    "The root-overflow test must be driven by `tail_offset` (elements in the trie), not `size`. Correct form: `(tail_offset >> BITS) + 1 > (1usize << shift)`. Using `size` mis-fires by one leaf and corrupts the tree at depth transitions.",
    "Test the exact depth-growth sizes, not just powers of 32. Depth grows when `tail_offset` hits `32^k`, i.e. at SIZE `32^k + 32`: 1056, 32800, 1048608, 33554464. Testing only 1024/32768 misses the bug entirely. My prototype matched the reference at 1055/1056/1057, 32799/32800/32801 and 1048607/1048608/1048609.",
    "`let toff = self.size - B;` in the tail-flush branch underflows if reached with a non-full tail. It is only correct under the `tail.len() == B` guard — keep the branch structure as written and never reorder the `tl < B` test.",
    "Do NOT deep-clone subtrees in the path copy. `node.inner().clone()` on a `Vec<Rc<Node<T>>>` clones 32 Rc HANDLES (refcount bumps), which is the whole point. If `Node` ever derives `Clone` structurally over `T`, an accidental `(*node).clone()` silently turns O(log n) into O(n) and destroys sharing. Prefer not deriving `Clone` on `Node` at all.",
    "Adopt the full tail into the trie by `self.tail.clone()` (Rc bump), NOT by cloning its `Vec<T>` into a new `Node::Leaf`. Copying costs 32 elements per flush for zero benefit, and storing the tail as `Rc<Node<T>>` (rather than `Rc<Vec<T>>`) is what makes the bump possible — this is also what immer does.",
    "Implement `IntoIterator` for `&Vector<T>`, not for `Vector<T>`. The program iterates `v` and then indexes it again afterward; a consuming iterator will not compile against that usage and would misrepresent the immutable contract.",
    "Never use `Rc::make_mut`/`Rc::get_mut` to mutate a node in place. `push_back` takes `&self`, so the receiver always holds a reference and the refcount is >= 1 — `make_mut` would copy anyway, but reaching for it invites a real aliasing bug the moment someone adds a `&mut self` fast path.",
    "Do not build the iterator as `(0..size).map(|i| v.get(i))`. It is correct but re-descends per element; measurably ~4x slower at 20M elements. Cache the leaf slice and refill only on the 1-in-32 boundary crossing.",
    "`std::stoi` semantics are NOT `str::parse::<i32>()`. Verified against the binary: leading ASCII whitespace is skipped (`" 7"`→7, `"\t5"`→5, `"\n6"`→6), an optional `+`/`-` sign is accepted (`"+9"`→9, `"-5"`→-5), parsing stops at the first non-digit WITHOUT error (`"12abc"`→12, `"0x10"`→0), no digits at all throws `invalid_argument` (`"abc"`, `""`), and out-of-int-range throws `out_of_range`. `str::parse` rejects `"12abc"` and `" 7"`, so hand-roll it. Accumulate in `i64` and range-check against `i32::MIN..=i32::MAX` (and treat digit runs long enough to overflow `i64` as out_of_range).",
    "Negative or zero n must produce `0\n0\n`, not a panic: the push loop simply does not execute and `0..v.size()` is empty. Confirmed `-1` and `-5` both print `0` `0` with exit 0. Extra trailing argv entries are ignored.",
    "For an uncaught-exception input, the reference writes to STDERR and aborts with shell-reported exit 134 (SIGABRT), printing exactly (note the two-space indents, byte-verified with `od -c`): `terminate called after throwing an instance of 'std::invalid_argument'\n  what():  stoi\n`. Emit that verbatim to stderr, write nothing to stdout, and exit 134 (`std::process::exit(134)`; or `std::process::abort()` if the harness inspects the real signal).",
    "`std::endl` flushes. Rust's stdout is line-buffered to a TTY but BLOCK-buffered to a pipe. `println!` twice then a normal exit still flushes, but if you ever swap in a `BufWriter` or call `process::exit` early, output can be lost — flush explicitly after each line to match `endl`.",
    "Recursion depth in `push_leaf`, `new_path`, and the implicit `Drop` of `Rc<Node<T>>` is bounded by tree depth (<= 13 even for usize::MAX elements), so there is NO stack-overflow risk here — unlike a persistent linked list. Do not over-engineer an iterative drop.",
    "Per-node `Vec` costs three words (ptr/len/cap) plus a separate allocation; that is the entire ~2.5x gap I measured versus the C++ reference at 20M elements. If that matters, switch node payloads to `Box<[T]>`/`Box<[Rc<Node<T>>]>` (drops the cap word) — but only after the differential tests pass, since it complicates the `push` path."
  ],
  "rust_1_75_concerns": [
    "Everything in this design is stable well before 1.75 — `Rc`, enums, slices, `wrapping_*`, `ExactSizeIterator`, `impl Index`. I compiled and ran the full prototype under the exact toolchain present here (`rustc 1.75.0 (82e1608df 2023-12-21)`) with no feature gates and no warnings.",
    "Do NOT reach for `impl Trait` in associated type position / RPITIT for the iterator — not stable in 1.75. Use the explicit named `Iter<'a, T>` struct with `type IntoIter = Iter<'a, T>` as specified.",
    "Avoid `let ... else` chained with complex patterns and any `async fn` in traits; neither is needed. `let else` itself IS stable in 1.75 (since 1.65) if you want it for the stoi parser.",
    "No GATs, no const generics beyond trivial array sizes, no `Rc::new_cyclic` — the trie is a DAG with no cycles, so `Rc` (not `Arc`, not `Weak`) is exactly right for a single-threaded program. `Rc` also avoids atomic refcount traffic, which matters given ~40M refcount ops at n=20M.",
    "`Vector<T>` is intentionally NOT `Send`/`Sync` because `Rc` is not. The C++ program is single-threaded, so this is a faithful match (immer's default memory policy also uses non-atomic refcounting unless configured otherwise). Do not switch to `Arc` 'just in case' — it is a measurable regression for zero benefit here.",
    "`1usize << self.shift` is safe for all reachable states: with n bounded by `i32::MAX`, `shift` never exceeds 25, far below the 63-bit shift-overflow panic threshold. Still worth a `debug_assert!(self.shift < usize::BITS)` to document it.",
    "`Vec::clone()` on the node payload requires `T: Clone`; put that bound on the `impl` blocks that need it (`push_back`, `leaf_for`, `iter`) rather than on the struct definition, so `Vector<T>` stays usable for non-Clone `T` where possible. For this program `T = i32`, which is `Copy`.",
    "Keep `unsafe` at exactly zero. The borrow checker accepts the `leaf_for -> &'a [T]` pattern natively because `&'a self` transitively keeps every `Rc` on the descent path alive; no raw pointers or lifetime transmutes are needed anywhere in this design."
