- All returned references are reborrows `&'a Rc<Node<T>> -> &'a Node<T>`, so `&'a T` is sound with ZERO `unsafe` anywhere in the module.
- Cost: one shift+mask per level, ≤ 5 levels at n = 10^7 and ≤ 13 worst case; no allocation, no recursion.",
  "iteration_algorithm": "Chunk-caching forward cursor — O(1) amortized per element, no `unsafe`, no per-element tree descent:

```rust
pub struct Iter<'a, T> {
    vec: &'a Vector<T>,
    idx: usize,          // next index to yield
    leaf: &'a [T],       // cached chunk covering [leaf_base, leaf_base + leaf.len())
    leaf_base: usize,    // multiple of WIDTH; init to usize::MAX so the first next() always refreshes
}
impl<'a, T> Iterator for Iter<'a, T> {
    type Item = &'a T;
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a T> {
        if self.idx >= self.vec.size { return None; }
        if self.leaf_base != (self.idx & !MASK) {            // crossed a 32-boundary
            self.leaf_base = self.idx & !MASK;
            self.leaf = self.vec.leaf_for(self.idx);
        }
        let out = self.leaf.get(self.idx - self.leaf_base);   // Option, never unchecked
        self.idx += 1;
        out
    }
    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) { let n = self.vec.size - self.idx; (n, Some(n)) }
}
impl<'a, T> ExactSizeIterator for Iter<'a, T> {}
```
- Correct in the tail region because `tail_off` is a multiple of WIDTH, so `idx & !MASK` is the right base on both sides of `tail_off`. Put that sentence as a comment at the `& !MASK`.
